":"Bach’s Prelude opens The Well-Tempered Clavier, Book I (1722); Gounod later laid his “Ave Maria” melody over it.","difficulty":2,"audio":{"melody":"C4:.25 E4:.25 G4:.25 C5:.25 E5:.25 G4:.25 C5:.25 E5:.25 C4:.25 E4:.25 G4:.25 C5:.25 E5:.25 G4:.25 C5:.25 E5:.25 | C4:.25 D4:.25 A4:.25 D5:.25 F5:.25 A4:.25 D5:.25 F5:.25 C4:.25 D4:.25 A4:.25 D5:.25 F5:.25 A4:.25 D5:.25 F5:.25 | B3:.25 D4:.25 G4:.25 D5:.25 F5:.25 G4:.25 D5:.25 F5:.25 B3:.25 D4:.25 G4:.25 D5:.25 F5:.25 G4:.25 D5:.25 F5:.25 | C4:.25 E4:.25 G4:.25 C5:.25 E5:.25 G4:.25 C5:.25 E5:.25 C4:.25 E4:.25 G4:.25 C5:.25 E5:.25 G4:.25 C5:.25 E5:.25","tempo":84,"voice":"harpsichord"}},{"id":"minuet-in-g","prompt":"♪","answer":"Minuet in G","alts":["Minuet in G major","Petzold Minuet"],"options":["Prelude in C major","Canon in D","Eine kleine Nachtmusik"],"fact":"Long credited to J. S. Bach, it is now attributed to Christian Petzold; it survives in the 1725 notebook Bach kept for his wife, Anna Magdalena.","difficulty":3,"audio":{"melody":"D5:1 G4:.5 A4:.5 B4:.5 C5:.5 | D5:1 G4:1 G4:1 | E5:1 C5:.5 D5:.5 E5:.5 F#5:.5 | G5:1 G4:1 G4:1 | C5:1 D5:.5 C5:.5 B4:.5 A4:.5 | B4:1 C5:.5 B4:.5 A4:.5 G4:.5 | F#4:1 G4:.5 A4:.5 B4:.5 G4:.5 | A4:3 || G3+B3+D4:2 A3:1 | B3:3 | C4:3 | B3:3 | A3:3 | G3:3 | D4:1 B3:1 G3:1 | D3:3","tempo":132,"voice":"harpsichord"}},{"id":"canon-in-d","prompt":"♪","answer":"Canon in D","alts":["Pachelbel's Canon","Canon and Gigue"],"fact":"Johann Pachelbel wrote it around 1700; it was forgotten for centuries, then became the wedding processional of the late 20th century.","difficulty":1,"audio":{"melody":"F#5:2 E5:2 | D5:2 C#5:2 | B4:2 A4:2 | B4:2 C#5:2 || D3:2 A2:2 | B2:2 F#2:2 | G2:2 D2:2 | G2:2 A2:2","tempo":80,"voice":"strings"}},{"id":"vivaldi-spring","prompt":"♪","answer":"Spring","alts":["La primavera","The Four Seasons: Spring","Spring from The Four Seasons"],"fact":"The first of Vivaldi’s Four Seasons (published 1725); each concerto came with a sonnet describing the scene — here, birdsong and spring breezes.","difficulty":2,"audio":{"melody":"E5:.5 | G#5:.5 G#5:.5 G#5:.5 F#5:.25 E5:.25 B5:1.5 B5:.25 A5:.25 | G#5:.5 G#5:.5 G#5:.5 F#5:.25 E5:.25 B5:2 || R:.5 | E3+B3:1 E3+B3:1 E3+B3:1 E3+B3:1 | E3+B3:1 E3+B3:1 E3+B3:2","tempo":92,"voice":"strings"}},{"id":"hallelujah-chorus","prompt":"♪","answer":"Hallelujah Chorus","alts":["Hallelujah","Handel's Hallelujah"],"fact":"From Handel’s oratorio Messiah (1741); by tradition audiences stand for it, supposedly because King George II once did.","difficulty":1,"audio":{"melody":"D4+F#4+A4+D5:1 D4+F#4+A4:.75 D4+G4+B4:.25 D4+F#4+A4:1 R:1 | D4+F#4+A4+D5:1 D4+F#4+A4:.75 D4+G4+B4:.25 D4+F#4+A4:1 R:1 || D2+D3:1 D3:1 D2+D3:1 R:1 | D2+D3:1 D3:1 D2+D3:1 R:1","tempo":72,"voice":"organ"}},{"id":"hall-of-the-mountain-king","prompt":"♪","answer":"In the Hall of the Mountain King","alts":["Hall of the Mountain King"],"options":["Morning Mood","Beethoven's Symphony No. 5","Can-can"],"fact":"Grieg wrote it for Ibsen’s play Peer Gynt (1875); the theme creeps in quietly and accelerates into a frenzy.","difficulty":1,"audio":{"melody":"B3:.5 C#4:.5 D4:.5 E4:.5 F#4:.5 D4:.5 F#4:1 | F4:.5 C#4:.5 F4:1 E4:.5 C4:.5 E4:1 | B3:.5 C#4:.5 D4:.5 E4:.5 F#4:.5 D4:.5 F#4:.5 B4:.5 | A4:.5 F#4:.5 D4:.5 F#4:.5 A4:2 || B1+B2:.5 R:.5 F#2:.5 R:.5 B1+B2:.5 R:.5 F#2:.5 R:.5 B1+B2:.5 R:.5 F#2:.5 R:.5 B1+B2:.5 R:.5 F#2:.5 R:.5 B1+B2:.5 R:.5 F#2:.5 R:.5 B1+B2:.5 R:.5 F#2:.5 R:.5 B1+B2:.5 R:.5 F#2:.5 R:.5 B1+B2:.5 R:.5 F#2:.5 R:.5","tempo":116,"voice":"piano"}},{"id":"morning-mood","prompt":"♪","answer":"Morning Mood","alts":["Morning","Peer Gynt: Morning"],"options":["In the Hall of the Mountain King","Spring","Swan Lake"],"fact":"Also from Grieg’s Peer Gynt (1875); despite its Nordic feel, it depicts sunrise over the Moroccan desert.","difficulty":2,"audio":{"melody":"G#5:1 E5:.5 D#5:1 C#5:.5 | D#5:1 E5:.5 G#5:1 E5:.5 | D#5:1 C#5:.5 D#5:.5 E5:.5 D#5:.5 | E5:.5 G#5:.5 E5:.5 G#5:.5 A5:.5 E5:.5 | A5:.5 G#5:.5 E5:.5 D#5:.5 C#5:1 || E3+B3:3 | E3+B3:3 | E3+B3:3 | E3+B3:3 | E3+B3:3","tempo":132,"voice":"organ"}},{"id":"swan-lake","prompt":"♪","answer":"Swan Lake","alts":["Swan Lake theme","Scene from Swan Lake"],"fact":"Tchaikovsky’s first ballet (1876); its premiere was a flop, and it only became a classic after his death.","difficulty":1,"audio":{"melody":"F#5:3 B4:.5 C#5:.5 D5:.5 E5:.5 | F#5:1.5 D5:.5 F#5:1.5 D5:.5 | F#5:1.5 B4:.5 D5:.5 B4:.5 G4:.5 D5:.5 | B4:3 || B2+F#3:4 B2+F#3:4 | B2+F#3:2 G2+D3:2 | B2+F#3:4","tempo":84,"voice":"strings"}},{"id":"blue-danube","prompt":"♪","answer":"The Blue Danube","alts":["Blue Danube","An der schönen blauen Donau","Blue Danube Waltz"],"fact":"Johann Strauss II wrote the waltz in 1866; a century later it floated spacecraft through 2001: A Space Odyssey.","difficulty":1,"audio":{"melody":"D4:1 | D4:1 F#4:1 A4:1 | A4:3 | R:1 A5:1 A5:1 | R:1 F#5:1 F#5:1 | R:2 D4:1 | D4:1 F#4:1 A4:1 | A4:3 | R:1 A5:1 A5:1 | R:1 G5:1 G5:1 || R:1 | D3:1 F#3+A3:1 F#3+A3:1 D3:1 F#3+A3:1 F#3+A3:1 D3:1 F#3+A3:1 F#3+A3:1 D3:1 F#3+A3:1 F#3+A3:1 D3:1 F#3+A3:1 F#3+A3:1 D3:1 F#3+A3:1 F#3+A3:1 D3:1 F#3+A3:1 F#3+A3:1 | A2:1 C#3+G3:1 C#3+G3:1 A2:1 C#3+G3:1 C#3+G3:1","tempo":168,"voice":"strings"}},{"id":"can-can","prompt":"♪","answer":"Can-can","alts":["Galop infernal","Infernal Galop","Cancan"],"fact":"Offenbach’s “Infernal Galop” from the operetta Orpheus in the Underworld (1858), forever linked with the can-can dance.","difficulty":1,"audio":{"melody":"C5:1 D5:.5 F5:.5 E5:.5 D5:.5 G5:1 | G5:1 G5:.5 A5:.5 E5:.5 F5:.5 D5:1 | D5:1 D5:.5 F5:.5 E5:.5 D5:.5 | C5:.5 C6:.5 B5:.5 A5:.5 G5:.5 F5:.5 E5:.5 D5:.5","tempo":144,"voice":"piano"}},{"id":"habanera","prompt":"♪","answer":"Habanera","alts":["L'amour est un oiseau rebelle","Habanera from Carmen"],"fact":"From Bizet’s Carmen (1875); Bizet borrowed the tune from a song by Sebastián Yradier, believing it was a folk melody.","difficulty":2,"audio":{"melody":"R:4 | D5:.75 C#5:.25 C5:.5 C5:.5 | B4:.75 Bb4:.25 A4:.5 A4:.5 | A4:2 || D3:.75 A3:.25 F3:.5 A3:.5 D3:.75 A3:.25 F3:.5 A3:.5